---
/**
 * Nepali text-to-speech, one fixed female voice.
 *
 * The second model in the project, and a useful contrast to the cloning studio:
 * same language, same romanised text front-end, completely different trade-off.
 * It was its own Flask app on its own Vercel project until it was folded in here --
 * the Flask layer rendered a single static template and nothing else.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import TtsStudio from '../components/tts/TtsStudio.astro';
import TtsResults from '../components/tts/TtsResults.astro';

const facts = [
  { dt: 'Voice', dd: 'One', sub: ', fixed' },
  { dt: 'Synthesizer', dd: 'Tacotron2', sub: '' },
  { dt: 'Vocoder', dd: 'HiFi-GAN', sub: '' },
  { dt: 'Output rate', dd: '22.05', sub: 'kHz' },
];
---
<Base
  title="Nepali text-to-speech — Nepali Voice Cloning"
  description="Nepali text-to-speech in a single female voice: a Tacotron2 synthesizer vocoded with HiFi-GAN at 22.05 kHz, running live on CPU."
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <main id="main">
    <section class="intro">
      <div class="shell">
        <p class="eyebrow">Tacotron2 &middot; HiFi-GAN &middot; one voice</p>
        <h1>Nepali text-to-speech,<br /><em>one fixed voice.</em></h1>
        <p class="lede">
          The other model on this site clones whoever you give it. This one does the
          opposite: a single female voice, baked in, reading whatever you type.
        </p>

        <div class="facts">
          <dl>
            {facts.map((f) => (
              <div><dt>{f.dt}</dt><dd>{f.dd}<span>{f.sub}</span></dd></div>
            ))}
          </dl>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <TtsStudio />
    <TtsResults />

    <section class="notes">
      <div class="shell">
        <header class="head">
          <p class="eyebrow">Under the hood</p>
          <h2>How it differs.</h2>
        </header>

        <div class="grid">
          <article>
            <h3>It is <em>not</em> the cloning model</h3>
            <p>
              Different architecture, different Space, different checkpoint. Tacotron2
              rather than Tacotron, and HiFi-GAN rather than WaveRNN. The two demos
              share this site and a text front-end; they share no weights.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <h3>Genuinely single-speaker</h3>
            <p>
              There is no speaker embedding anywhere in this model &mdash; the decoder
              takes the text and nothing else. The voice is a property of the
              checkpoint, which is why there is no reference clip to choose and no way
              to ask it for a different voice.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <h3>The vocoder is not the wall here</h3>
            <p>
              HiFi-GAN is non-autoregressive: it produces the whole waveform in one
              pass instead of sampling 16,000 values per second in sequence. That is
              why this answers in seconds where the cloning studio, on comparable
              hardware, takes considerably longer for the same length of speech.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <h3>Romanised, like the other one</h3>
            <p>
              The same constraint applies: the symbol set is ASCII, <code>unidecode</code>
              runs before the network sees anything, and digits are dropped rather than
              spoken. The keyboards above are aids for you, not for the model.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <h3>The decoder does not always stop</h3>
            <p>
              Tacotron2 emits a stop token when it thinks the utterance is finished. When
              that gate fails to fire it runs to its 1000-step ceiling instead, which is
              about 11.6&nbsp;s of audio regardless of how short the input was &mdash; the
              speech, then artefact. Watch the alignment panel: a diagonal that runs flat
              into the right-hand edge is this happening.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <h3>The plots are drawn here, not there</h3>
            <p>
              The server sends mel and alignment as raw arrays and your browser draws
              them, which is why they follow the theme. It used to render them as
              matplotlib PNGs &mdash; roughly 29% of a 705&nbsp;KB response &mdash; and
              rebuild both networks from disk on every request while it was at it.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</Base>

<script>
  import '../scripts/tts.ts';
</script>

<style>
  .intro { padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 5rem) clamp(1rem, 2vw, 1.5rem); }
  h1 { font-size: var(--step-4); max-width: 16ch; margin-block: 0.5em 0.5em; }
  h1 em { font-style: italic; color: var(--accent); }
  .lede { color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 56ch; font-size: var(--step-1); line-height: 1.5; }

  .facts { margin-top: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem); }
  dl {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(9.5rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1px;
    margin: 0;
    background: var(--rule);
    border-block: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  dl > div { background: var(--paper); padding: 1rem 0.2rem 1.05rem; }
  dt {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
  }
  dd {
    margin: 0.35rem 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    line-height: 1;
  }
  dd span {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }

  .notes { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem); background: var(--paper-sunk); }
  .head { margin-bottom: clamp(1.8rem, 3vw, 2.6rem); }
  h2 { font-size: var(--step-3); margin-top: 0.4em; }
  .grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 2.2rem) clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(17rem, 1fr));
  }
  article { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); padding-top: 1rem; }
  h3 { font-size: var(--step-1); margin-bottom: 0.55rem; }
  h3 em { font-style: italic; color: var(--accent); }
  .grid p { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: var(--step--1); line-height: 1.65; }
  code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.88em;
    background: var(--paper);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding: 0.05em 0.32em;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }
</style>

<style is:global>
  body { display: flex; flex-direction: column; min-height: 100vh; }
  main { flex: 1; }
</style>
